OSKit Arch -- x86_64 CPU Primitives

Architecture-specific CPU operations for x86_64: interrupt
enable/disable, and thread stack frame construction.

cli/sti are extern (provided by boot.s as bare instruction
+ ret) to avoid Sysl function prologue/epilogue overhead.

    module oskit.arch

    extern arch_vm_copy_to_demand(dst_ptbr: u64, dst_vaddr: u64, src: *byte, len: int) -> int

Offset from saved SSP to the return-value register (RAX)
in the context frame. Used by syscall_return to write the
result where restore_context will pop it into RAX.
x86_64 layout: R15(0)..RBX(104)..RAX(112)..RIP(120)..

    val SYSCALL_RETURN_OFFSET = 112
    val STACK_FRAME_SIZE = 160       // 20 fields × 8 bytes (15 GP regs + 5 iretq)

Frame offsets used by `execve` (and any future exec-shape primitive) to
overwrite the calling thread's saved instruction-pointer / user-stack
slots. RIP and RSP land in the iretq frame at offsets 120 and 144 from
SSP — see `build_user_stack_frame` above for the full layout. Declared
as `const` so the compiler folds the literal at every use site (matches
the aarch64 sibling).

    const ARCH_FRAME_PC_OFFSET  = 120
    const ARCH_FRAME_USP_OFFSET = 144

No-ops on x86: the architecture guarantees cache coherency between
data and instruction fetches automatically (a serializing
instruction such as `iret` or `mov to cr3` flushes the prefetch
queue when needed). Provided so `sys_execve_finalize_handler` and
similar can call them unconditionally without arch-specific guards.
Implemented as bare `ret` C stubs in `stubs.c`.

    extern arch_invalidate_icache_all()
    extern arch_sync_icache_range(start: i64, len: int)

Disable interrupts (x86 cli instruction).

    extern arch_cli()

Enable interrupts (x86 sti instruction).

    extern arch_sti()

Set x86 hardware watchpoint on a 4-byte address (uses DR0).
CPU raises #DB on any write to the watched address.

    extern set_hw_watchpoint(addr: u64)

Save and restore IRQ state for nestable critical sections.
arch_irq_save reads RFLAGS, disables interrupts (cli), and
returns the previous flags. arch_irq_restore writes the
saved flags back, which re-enables interrupts iff IF was
set when the save happened. Provided by stubs.c so the
inline asm doesn't fight the Sysl prologue.

    extern arch_irq_save() -> u64
    extern arch_irq_restore(prev: u64)

Read / write the per-thread TLS pointer register (FS_BASE, MSR
0xC0000100). The scheduler calls these on every context switch so
user threads sharing one address space can hold distinct thread-
local pointers. CR4.FSGSBASE is enabled in boot.s so both ring 3
and ring 0 can use RDFSBASE / WRFSBASE without trapping. Userspace
sets FS_BASE directly via the musl `__set_thread_area` override;
the kernel never reads or writes it outside these hooks.

    extern arch_read_tls() -> i64
    extern arch_write_tls(v: i64)

Spinlock acquire. Spins using lock cmpxchg until the lock
is obtained, then disables interrupts. Lock is 0=free, 1=held.
x86 calling convention: rdi = first arg (lock pointer).

    arch_spinlock_acquire(lock: *i64)
        asm("movq $$1, %rsi\n.spin_acq:\nxorq %rax, %rax\nlock cmpxchgq %rsi, (%rdi)\njnz .spin_acq\ncli")

Spinlock release. Enables interrupts, then stores 0 to lock.

    arch_spinlock_release(lock: *i64)
        asm("sti\nmovq $$0, (%rdi)")

Update TSS.RSP0 for the next ring 3 thread. Called by the
scheduler on context switch. Provided by boot.s.

    extern tss_set_rsp0(rsp0: i64)

Build a fake interrupt frame on the supervisor stack so
that restore_context in boot.s can pop it and iretq into
the new thread. x86_64 layout (20 fields, bottom to top):

15 GP regs: R15..R14..R13..R12..R11..R10..R9..R8..RBP..RDI..RSI..RDX..RCX..RBX..RAX
iretq frame: RIP, CS, RFLAGS, RSP, SS

The thread's user stack gets thread_exit as a return address
so that a thread function returning normally lands in the
exit trampoline.

    extern thread_exit: byte

    build_stack_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64) -> i64
        // Place thread_exit as return address on user stack
        val ret_sp = usp - 8
        *(*i64(ret_sp)) = i64(&thread_exit)

        var sp = ssp
        // iretq frame (what the CPU would push on interrupt)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0x10)         // SS  (kernel data segment)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = ret_sp            // RSP (user stack, with return addr)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0x202)        // RFLAGS (IF=1, reserved bit 1)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0x08)         // CS  (kernel code segment)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = entry_point       // RIP
        // 15 GP registers (all zero)
        var i = 0
        while i < 15
            sp -= 8
            *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0)
            i += 1
        sp

Build a ring 3 stack frame on the per-process kernel stack.
Used by create_process_suspended for user processes. The frame
is built directly on the kernel stack (identity-mapped), not
in the child's address space. Does NOT write thread_exit to
usp — the caller handles that via vm_copy_to.

On iretq, the CPU detects CS RPL=3 and switches to ring 3,
loading SS:RSP from the frame (user stack).

    build_user_stack_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64) -> i64
        var sp = ssp
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0x23)         // SS  (user data, RPL=3)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = usp               // RSP (user stack) — points at SysV
                                        // init frame that PM built at `usp`.
                                        // aarch64 sets SP_EL0 = usp similarly.
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0x202)        // RFLAGS (IF=1)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0x1B)         // CS  (user code, RPL=3)
        sp -= 8
        *(*i64(sp)) = entry_point       // RIP
        var i = 0
        while i < 15
            sp -= 8
            *(*i64(sp)) = i64(0)
            i += 1
        sp

Set up the initial context frame for a new process. On x86_64,
the context frame is built directly on the per-process kernel
stack (identity-mapped). The CPU switches to this stack via
TSS.RSP0 on ring 3 to ring 0 transitions. Returns the SSP value.

    arch_setup_process_frame(entry_point: i64, usp: i64, ssp: i64, kstack_top_val: i64, ptbr_val: u64) -> i64
        build_user_stack_frame(entry_point, usp, kstack_top_val)
        kstack_top_val - STACK_FRAME_SIZE

Build a `fork()` continuation frame on the child's kernel stack.
The child must resume at the *same* user-mode PC as the parent's
post-syscall instruction with all GP regs preserved — except the
syscall return register (RAX on x86_64), which userspace reads as
the fork return value. Standard POSIX semantics: child sees 0,
parent sees the child's pid.

We achieve that by byte-copying the parent's saved exception
frame from `parent_ssp` onto the child's kstack, then zeroing the
RAX slot in the copy. The kstacks are identity-mapped under both
the parent's PT and any kernel-only PTBR, so a plain byte copy
crosses the address-space boundary cleanly.

The 160-byte layout (15 GP regs at 0..120, then iretq frame
RIP/CS/RFLAGS/RSP/SS at 120..160) matches `build_stack_frame` and
`build_user_stack_frame` exactly, which is what `restore_context`
in `boot.s` expects. After scheduling picks up the child, that
restore unwinds straight back to ring 3 at the post-syscall RIP
with RSP = parent's user RSP and RAX = 0.

Returns the SSP value to store in the child thread (frame base).

    arch_setup_fork_frame(parent_ssp: i64, kstack_top_val: i64) -> i64
        val frame = kstack_top_val - STACK_FRAME_SIZE
        var i = 0
        while i < STACK_FRAME_SIZE
            var src: *byte = *byte(parent_ssp + i64(i))
            var dst: *byte = *byte(frame + i64(i))
            *dst = *src
            i += 1
        var rv: *i64 = *i64(frame + i64(SYSCALL_RETURN_OFFSET))
        *rv = i64(0)
        frame


`arch_trap_at_user_mode` reports whether the iretq trap frame at
`ssp` records a ring-3 entry (returns 1) or a ring-0 entry
(returns 0). The signal walker uses this to gate handler delivery
so a thread that took an INT $0x80 from kernel mode (eg.
`sleep_or_notify` called by `sys_ppoll`) doesn't get a sigframe
armed on its inner ring-0 frame — that would iretq back into
ring 0 with RIP set to a user handler and immediately page-fault.

    arch_trap_at_user_mode(ssp: i64) -> int
        val saved_cs_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(128))
        val saved_cs = *saved_cs_p
        if (saved_cs & i64(0x3)) == i64(0x3)
            return 1
        0


Signal-frame construction for POSIX signal delivery
---------------------------------------------------

`arch_build_sigframe` lays down an x86_64 `rt_sigframe`
(`pretcode`, `ucontext_t`, `siginfo_t`) on the target thread's
user stack and rewrites the saved iretq trap frame so the next
iretq returns into the userspace signal handler with:

  - RDI = signo, RSI = &siginfo, RDX = &ucontext
  - RIP = handler, RSP = new frame base (pointing at pretcode)

The handler returns normally via `ret`, which pops `pretcode` (the
libc `__restore_rt` trampoline) into RIP. The trampoline then
issues SYS_RT_SIGRETURN to undo the frame.

User-side frame layout (Linux x86_64 ABI):

```
new_sp + 0     pretcode (8B = libc __restore_rt pointer)
new_sp + 8     ucontext_t (424B):
                 +0    uc_flags        (u64, 0)
                 +8    uc_link         (u64, 0)
                 +16   uc_stack        (24B, zeroed)
                 +40   uc_mcontext (256B):
                         +0    gregs[23]      (R8..CR2, 184B)
                         +184  fpregs (ptr)   (8B, 0)
                         +192  __reserved[8]  (64B, 0)
                 +296  uc_sigmask      (128B; low 8B = saved kernel-shape mask)
new_sp + 432   siginfo_t (128B, only si_signo non-zero)
```

Total frame = 560 bytes. `new_sp` is chosen so that `(new_sp + 8)`
is 16-byte aligned (SysV ABI for entry via simulated CALL).

`gregs[]` order mirrors Linux's `struct ucontext`:
`R8 R9 R10 R11 R12 R13 R14 R15 RDI RSI RBP RBX RDX RAX RCX RSP
RIP EFL CSGSFS ERR TRAPNO OLDMASK CR2`. Trap-frame slot offsets
on SLIX are: R15(0) R14(8) R13(16) R12(24) R11(32) R10(40)
R9(48) R8(56) RBP(64) RDI(72) RSI(80) RDX(88) RCX(96) RBX(104)
RAX(112) RIP(120) CS(128) RFLAGS(136) RSP(144) SS(152).

ERR/TRAPNO/OLDMASK/CR2 stay zero; SLIX doesn't propagate fault
detail through ucontext yet. CSGSFS is packed as low-16 = CS.

The 560-byte staging buffer is module-scoped and reused across
deliveries; the walker is non-reentrant on a single-CPU kernel.

    var _sigframe_buf: [560]byte

    arch_build_sigframe(ssp: i64, ptbr: u64, signo: int,
                        handler: i64, restorer: i64,
                        sa_mask: i64, sa_flags: i32,
                        old_sigmask: i64) -> i64
        val saved_rsp_p:    *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(144))
        val saved_rip_p:    *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(120))
        val saved_rflags_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(136))
        val saved_cs_p:     *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(128))
        val saved_rsp    = *saved_rsp_p
        val saved_rip    = *saved_rip_p
        val saved_rflags = *saved_rflags_p
        val saved_cs     = *saved_cs_p

        var z = 0
        while z < 560
            _sigframe_buf[z] = byte(0)
            z += 1

        val buf = i64(&_sigframe_buf[0])

        var pretcode_p: *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(0))
        *pretcode_p = restorer

        var sigmask_p: *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(304))
        *sigmask_p = old_sigmask

        var g0_p:  *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 0  * 8))
        var g1_p:  *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 1  * 8))
        var g2_p:  *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 2  * 8))
        var g3_p:  *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 3  * 8))
        var g4_p:  *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 4  * 8))
        var g5_p:  *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 5  * 8))
        var g6_p:  *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 6  * 8))
        var g7_p:  *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 7  * 8))
        var g8_p:  *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 8  * 8))
        var g9_p:  *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 9  * 8))
        var g10_p: *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 10 * 8))
        var g11_p: *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 11 * 8))
        var g12_p: *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 12 * 8))
        var g13_p: *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 13 * 8))
        var g14_p: *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 14 * 8))
        var g15_p: *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 15 * 8))
        var g16_p: *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 16 * 8))
        var g17_p: *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 17 * 8))
        var g18_p: *i64 = *i64(buf + i64(48 + 18 * 8))

        val r8_p:  *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(56))
        val r9_p:  *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(48))
        val r10_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(40))
        val r11_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(32))
        val r12_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(24))
        val r13_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(16))
        val r14_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(8))
        val r15_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0))
        val rdi_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(72))
        val rsi_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(80))
        val rbp_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(64))
        val rbx_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(104))
        val rdx_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(88))
        val rax_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(112))
        val rcx_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(96))

        *g0_p  = *r8_p
        *g1_p  = *r9_p
        *g2_p  = *r10_p
        *g3_p  = *r11_p
        *g4_p  = *r12_p
        *g5_p  = *r13_p
        *g6_p  = *r14_p
        *g7_p  = *r15_p
        *g8_p  = *rdi_p
        *g9_p  = *rsi_p
        *g10_p = *rbp_p
        *g11_p = *rbx_p
        *g12_p = *rdx_p
        *g13_p = *rax_p
        *g14_p = *rcx_p
        *g15_p = saved_rsp
        *g16_p = saved_rip
        *g17_p = saved_rflags
        *g18_p = saved_cs & i64(0xFFFF)

        _sigframe_buf[432] = byte(signo & 0xFF)
        _sigframe_buf[433] = byte((signo >> 8) & 0xFF)
        _sigframe_buf[434] = byte((signo >> 16) & 0xFF)
        _sigframe_buf[435] = byte((signo >> 24) & 0xFF)

        val new_sp = ((saved_rsp - i64(552)) & ~i64(15)) - i64(8)
        arch_vm_copy_to_demand(ptbr, u64(new_sp), &_sigframe_buf[0], 560)

        val siginfo_addr = new_sp + i64(432)
        val uctx_addr    = new_sp + i64(8)

        var tf_rdi_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(72))
        var tf_rsi_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(80))
        var tf_rdx_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(88))
        var tf_rip_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(120))
        var tf_rsp_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(144))
        *tf_rdi_p = i64(signo)
        *tf_rsi_p = siginfo_addr
        *tf_rdx_p = uctx_addr
        *tf_rip_p = handler
        *tf_rsp_p = new_sp

        var new_mask = old_sigmask | sa_mask
        if (sa_flags & i32(0x40000000)) == i32(0)
            new_mask = new_mask | (i64(1) << (signo - 1))
        new_mask


Sigreturn — restore a user trap frame from `ucontext`
-----------------------------------------------------

Inverse of `arch_build_sigframe`. After the handler returns, its
`ret` pops `pretcode` (the restorer) into RIP and bumps RSP by 8.
The restorer issues SYS_RT_SIGRETURN with RSP pointing exactly at
the `ucontext_t` we previously staged, so the user RSP saved in
the trap frame *is* `&ucontext`.

`arch_restore_sigframe` reads `gregs[]`, RIP, EFLAGS, and RSP
out of `uc_mcontext` and writes them back into the saved iretq
trap frame so the next iretq resumes the interrupted code with
its full register state. RAX is **not** written into the trap
frame — it is returned to the caller and the shim dispatcher
stores it via its usual "syscall result" write.

`out_sigmask` receives `uc_sigmask` (low 8 kernel-shape bytes);
the caller copies it into `threads[tid].sigmask`.

`gregs[]` ordering matches Linux's x86_64 `mcontext.gregs`:
R8 R9 R10 R11 R12 R13 R14 R15 RDI RSI RBP RBX RDX RAX RCX RSP
RIP EFL CSGSFS ERR TRAPNO OLDMASK CR2. Indices 18..22 (CSGSFS
and friends) are ignored on restore — CS/SS stay kernel-set,
fault metadata isn't propagated.

    arch_restore_sigframe(ssp: i64, ptbr: u64, out_sigmask: *i64) -> i64
        val user_rsp_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(144))
        val user_rsp = *user_rsp_p
        val uctx_va = user_rsp

        var sigmask_bytes: [8]byte
        vm_copy_from(ptbr, u64(uctx_va + i64(296)), &sigmask_bytes[0], 8)
        val smp: *i64 = *i64(i64(&sigmask_bytes[0]))
        *out_sigmask = *smp

        var gregs: [184]byte
        vm_copy_from(ptbr, u64(uctx_va + i64(40)), &gregs[0], 184)

        val gbase = i64(&gregs[0])
        val g_r8_p:  *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(0  * 8))
        val g_r9_p:  *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(1  * 8))
        val g_r10_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(2  * 8))
        val g_r11_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(3  * 8))
        val g_r12_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(4  * 8))
        val g_r13_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(5  * 8))
        val g_r14_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(6  * 8))
        val g_r15_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(7  * 8))
        val g_rdi_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(8  * 8))
        val g_rsi_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(9  * 8))
        val g_rbp_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(10 * 8))
        val g_rbx_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(11 * 8))
        val g_rdx_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(12 * 8))
        val g_rax_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(13 * 8))
        val g_rcx_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(14 * 8))
        val g_rsp_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(15 * 8))
        val g_rip_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(16 * 8))
        val g_efl_p: *i64 = *i64(gbase + i64(17 * 8))

        var tf_r8_p:  *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(56))
        var tf_r9_p:  *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(48))
        var tf_r10_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(40))
        var tf_r11_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(32))
        var tf_r12_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(24))
        var tf_r13_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(16))
        var tf_r14_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(8))
        var tf_r15_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(0))
        var tf_rdi_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(72))
        var tf_rsi_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(80))
        var tf_rbp_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(64))
        var tf_rbx_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(104))
        var tf_rdx_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(88))
        var tf_rcx_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(96))
        var tf_rsp_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(144))
        var tf_rip_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(120))
        var tf_efl_p: *i64 = *i64(ssp + i64(136))

        *tf_r8_p  = *g_r8_p
        *tf_r9_p  = *g_r9_p
        *tf_r10_p = *g_r10_p
        *tf_r11_p = *g_r11_p
        *tf_r12_p = *g_r12_p
        *tf_r13_p = *g_r13_p
        *tf_r14_p = *g_r14_p
        *tf_r15_p = *g_r15_p
        *tf_rdi_p = *g_rdi_p
        *tf_rsi_p = *g_rsi_p
        *tf_rbp_p = *g_rbp_p
        *tf_rbx_p = *g_rbx_p
        *tf_rdx_p = *g_rdx_p
        *tf_rcx_p = *g_rcx_p
        *tf_rsp_p = *g_rsp_p
        *tf_rip_p = *g_rip_p
        *tf_efl_p = *g_efl_p

        *g_rax_p
